---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **「where to watch korean drama」에 정직하게 답한다.** (`/where-to-watch`)
 *
 * ── 왜 (2026-08-23) ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * 제가 잰 K컬처 후보 35개 중 **가장 센 말**입니다 — 자동완성 1번째 · 제안 10줄.
 * 그런데 저는 이 말을 미뤄 두고 있었습니다. 넷플릭스가 «제공 여부»를 안 내기 때문입니다.
 *
 * ⭐ 반만 맞았습니다. 말할 수 있는 것이 하나 있습니다 —
 *   「어느 주에 어느 나라 10위에 들었다」면 **그 주에 그 나라에서 볼 수 있었다.**
 *   차트에 든 것은 제공의 **바닥값**이고, 이건 추론이 아니라 산수입니다.
 * ⛔ 뒤집으면 안 됩니다 — 안 들었다고 없었다는 뜻이 아닙니다. 10위 밖은 자료가 없습니다.
 * ⛔ 「볼 수 있다」(현재)로 안 씁니다. 「그때 볼 수 있었다」(과거)입니다.
 *
 * ⭐ 이 되받기 자체가 우리 자리입니다. 남들은 있는 척 답합니다. 우리는 넷플릭스가 **무엇을
 *   안 내는지**를 먼저 적고 그다음에 낼 수 있는 것을 냅니다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwMethodLimits from '../../components/KcwMethodLimits.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-where-to-watch.json';

const TITLE = 'Where to watch Korean drama: what Netflix actually tells us';
const DESC = 'Netflix publishes no availability data for any country. It does publish a weekly top 10 for '
  + 'each one — and a title on that list was, that week, watchable there. This page is that floor: the '
  + 'countries where each Korean title demonstrably could be watched, and the week it was.';

const 넓 = data.widest[0];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  spatialCoverage: `${data.countryCount} countries`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/where-to-watch">
  <h1>Where to watch Korean drama</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    <b>Netflix does not publish where anything can be watched.</b> Not by country, not by title, not
    anywhere. Every site that answers this question with a straight list is guessing, scraping, or
    quietly out of date &mdash; and we are not going to pretend otherwise.
  </p>

  <p class="warn">
    <b>Here is the one thing that can be said for certain.</b> If a title held a place on a
    country&rsquo;s weekly Netflix top 10 in a given week, then it <b>was available in that country
    that week</b>. That is arithmetic, not an estimate. It is a floor, and a floor is worth more than
    a guess.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>What that floor looks like</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Across {data.weekCount} weeks and {data.marketCount} countries, {data.titlesConsidered} Korean
      titles held at least one place somewhere. <b>{넓.title}</b> reached the most:
      {넓.countries} countries, from {넓.firstWeek} to {넓.lastWeek}.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Title</th><th>Kind</th><th class="num">Countries</th><th class="num">Weeks</th><th>Some of them</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.widest.map((t) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="nm"><a href={`/title/${t.slug}`}>{t.title}</a></td>
            <td class="fine">{t.type === 'TV' ? 'Series' : 'Film'}</td>
            <td class="num">{t.countries}</td>
            <td class="num">{t.weeks}</td>
            <td class="fine">{t.someCountries.join(', ')}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Was it watchable in your country?</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      By country, the Korean titles that held a place there &mdash; longest run first, up to
      {data.perCountryShown} each. <b>A title missing from your country is not a no.</b> It only
      means it never entered that country&rsquo;s top 10, and most of a catalogue never does.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      {data.countriesWithoutSlug === 0
        ? 'Every country named below also has a page of its own, linked from its name.'
        : `${data.countriesWithoutSlug} of these countries have no page of their own yet, so their names are plain text.`}
    </p>
    {data.byCountry.map((c) => (
      <div class="cty">
        <p class="nm">
          {c.slug ? <a href={`/market/${c.slug}`}>{c.name}</a> : <span>{c.name}</span>}
          <span class="fine"> &middot; {c.titles.length} shown</span>
        </p>
        <p class="row">
          {c.titles.map((t, i) => (
            <span>
              {i > 0 ? ' · ' : ''}
              <a href={`/title/${t.slug}`}>{t.title}</a>
              <span class="fine"> {t.first}</span>
            </span>
          ))}
        </p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What this is, and what it is not</h2>
    <p class="fine">{data.whatThisIs}</p>
    <p class="fine"><b>Not this.</b> {data.whatThisIsNot}</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to walk from here</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/by-country">Every country and how Korean its chart is</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/underrated">The ones that ran and ran, almost nowhere</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/firms">Who made them</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/titles">Every Korean title we hold</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/community">All the rooms</a> &middot;{' '}
      <a href="/netflix-top-10-korean-drama">Netflix top 10 Korean drama, series only</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwMethodLimits
    method={`Netflix's own weekly top 10 files, ${data.weekCount} weeks across ${data.marketCount} countries, ${data.weekFrom} to ${data.weekTo}. For each title we take every country where it held at least one weekly place, and the first and last week it did. A place on a country's list means the title was available in that country in that week — that is the only availability statement this data supports, and we make no other.`}
    limitation={`This is a floor, never a total, and it is historical rather than current: our data ends at ${data.weekTo}, so nothing here says what is available today. Netflix publishes only the top 10, so a title available in a country without ever charting there will be missing — read a blank as unmeasured, never as a no. Netflix also states no country of production, so which titles count as Korean is our inference.`}
  />

  <style>
    .lead { font-size: 1.08rem; }
    .warn { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: .7rem 1rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 2rem;
      font-size: .95rem; }
    h2 { font-size: 1.12rem; margin: 2.4rem 0 .3rem; }
    .sub { color: var(--muted); font-size: .92rem; margin: 0 0 .8rem; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .92rem; }
    th, td { text-align: left; padding: .4rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
    th.num, td.num { text-align: right; }
    .cty { margin: 0 0 .9rem; }
    .nm { margin: 0 0 .15rem; font-weight: 600; }
    .row { margin: 0; line-height: 1.9; font-size: .93rem; }
    .fine { color: var(--muted); font-size: .84rem; font-weight: 400; }
  </style>
</WikiTip>
